<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple's privacy manifest for the VelaWallet app target (spec 081 FR-014).

  DERIVED FROM A MEASUREMENT, not from a guess. `nm -u` over the archived
  arm64 device binary (Release, whole-module) says which required-reason APIs
  the shipped executable actually imports; only those are declared. What the
  measurement found, and nothing else:

      _OBJC_CLASS_$_NSUserDefaults              -> UserDefaults
      _stat  _fstat  _lstat  _NSFileModificationDate  -> FileTimestamp

  Deliberately NOT declared, because the binary does not import them:
  DiskSpace (no statfs/fstatfs, no NSURLVolume*Capacity keys), SystemBootTime
  (no mach_absolute_time; the one clock call is clock_gettime, which is not a
  required-reason API) and ActiveKeyboards.

  The same measurement runs on every archive — the "Privacy manifest" gate in
  .github/workflows/ios-package.yml — and fails the build if a category appears
  in the binary that is missing here. Re-run it, do not edit this file from
  memory.

  No pbxproj edit was needed and none was made: app-ios/VelaWallet/VelaWallet/
  is a PBXFileSystemSynchronizedRootGroup (objectVersion 77) owned by the
  VelaWallet target, so a file committed here is a bundled resource by
  existing. Verified, not assumed — the archive puts it at the .app root.

  Lottie ships its own manifest (Lottie_Lottie.bundle/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy,
  FileTimestamp/C617.1), which Xcode aggregates into the privacy report.
  VelaCoreKit does NOT: it is a STATIC xcframework, so the Rust core's imports
  are this file's responsibility.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- No tracking, no ATT prompt, no tracking SDK. The wallet has no
	     analytics, no crash reporter, no ad identifier and no third-party
	     measurement. An empty NSPrivacyTrackingDomains must still be present
	     when NSPrivacyTracking is false. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!-- What leaves the device in a form someone could keep.
	     Apple's definition of "collect" is transmission that outlives servicing
	     the request in real time, and the relay meets it: getvela.app/privacy
	     says it "receives your wallet address and each transaction you send",
	     logs the address and each operation hash, and keeps operations for one
	     hour to 14 days so it can retry them. That is one thing — a
	     self-custodial wallet's on-chain financial activity — so it is declared
	     once, as Other Financial Info.

	     Not used for tracking: never joined with data from other companies'
	     apps or sites, never sold, never used for ads. Purpose is
	     AppFunctionality: without sending the transaction there is no
	     transaction.

	     SETTLED 2026-09-23 by reading the services, not by preference.
	     `Linked` was false here, following the published policy's "We don't
	     ask for your name, email, phone number or any ID". That sentence is
	     true and beside the point: Apple's "linked to the user" means linked
	     to an ACCOUNT, and the wallet address is the account. The relay logs
	     the sender on every accepted submit and keeps the whole signed
	     operation for up to 14 days; the index stores the address, the keys
	     and the names the person typed, and then publishes them permanently
	     to Gnosis. The evidence, with file and line, is in
	     docs/store-submission/privacy-evidence.md.

	     So both types are declared, both Linked, neither tracking. The App
	     Store Connect form is still the founder's to sign, and it must say
	     the same thing this file does — a manifest that disagrees with the
	     nutrition label is itself a rejection reason. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherFinancialInfo</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- The wallet address as the account's identifier, plus the
			     passkey public key, credential id and the name the person
			     chose — all stored by the index and published on-chain. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- Required-reason APIs the BINARY imports. Measured, then declared. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- `_OBJC_CLASS_$_NSUserDefaults`, from the app's own Swift.
			     Core/VelaStore.swift:108 and
			     Features/Onboarding/Core/AccountStore.swift:27 each hold a
			     `UserDefaults` and read and write only keys under this app's
			     own `vela.` prefix — VelaStore.swift:170 calls that prefix the
			     ownership boundary. Nothing is read that another app or the
			     system wrote, and nothing is written anywhere another app could
			     read it, which is exactly what CA92.1 describes. No app group,
			     no NSUbiquitousKeyValueStore, no shared suite. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- `_stat`, `_fstat`, `_lstat`, `_NSFileModificationDate`.
			     Two sources, both inside the app container:
			       * Lottie reads the launch animations bundled with the app
			         (`_lstat`, `_NSFileModificationDate`); its own manifest
			         declares the same category and reason.
			       * Rust's `std` inside libvela_core_uniffi.a (`_stat`,
			         `_fstat`, `_lstat`) — attributed with llvm-nm to std's
			         codegen unit, which the linker pulls in whole. A static
			         archive links by object, so std's file-system imports come
			         along with whatever else in that object is reachable.
			     The app's own file use is one export written to
			     `FileManager.default.temporaryDirectory`
			     (Features/Documents/DocumentPorts.swift:132). Every path here
			     is a file in the app's own container, which is C617.1. No app
			     group container, no CloudKit container, and no file the user
			     picked outside the sandbox. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
